Based on the GIFs and the CodeSandbox, what’s happening is that the Carbon SelectableTile is maintaining its own internal “selected” bit and blindly toggling it on every click/keypress. In a true “controlled” scenario (where a parent component is managing multi-select + shift-select logic), the parent will compute the new props.selected value and re-render the tile with that. But because the tile’s own handleClick() immediately flips its internal selected, it can end up out of sync with the parent’s prop and – in the specific “shift-click range select” scenario – the last tile gets deselected even though the parent is asking it to be selected.

The fix is to remove the internal toggle in handleClick and handleKeyDown, and simply defer entirely to props.selected (via getDerivedStateFromProps) once the parent has updated. In other words, on click or Enter/Space we fire the callbacks (handleClick/handleKeyDown/onChange) but do not change internal state ourselves.

